// Partial-domain one-wayness (PD-OW) of a trapdoor permutation F on
// F.k-bit strings, split as n1 bits of s and F.k - n1 bits of t (games
// cannot state `requires`, so the second width is derived rather than
// passed).
//
// The notion is due to Fujisaki, Okamoto, Pointcheval and Stern (Crypto
// 2001 / J. Cryptology 2004), who introduced it to repair the OAEP proof
// after Shoup showed that one-wayness alone cannot suffice: given
// y = f(s || t) for uniform s and t, recover s. FOPS also define its SET
// version, where the adversary outputs a list of q candidates and succeeds
// if s is among them, with Succ^pd-ow(tau) >= Succ^s-pd-ow(q, tau) / q.
// Their OAEP reduction naturally produces a list (the adversary's
// H-queries), so it is proven against the set version and the 1/q_H
// factor is paid once at the end; the CertiCrypt proof of Barthe,
// Gregoire, Lakhnech and Zanella Beguelin (CT-RSA 2011) stops at the set
// version, as does this file.
//
// Stated as a Left/Right pair in the multi-query "Solve" form used
// throughout these examples for search assumptions (compare
// Group/CDH.game): the challenge is y = f(pk, sStar || tStar) for uniform
// sStar (n1 bits) and tStar; the adversary submits candidates to Solve.
//
//   Left:  Solve(s) returns s == sStar;  RevealT(s) returns tStar on a hit
//   Right: Solve(s) always returns false; RevealT(s) always returns 0
//
// Lemma (set PD-OW implies this pair). For any adversary B making q
// Solve/RevealT queries, |Pr[B^Left = 1] - Pr[B^Right = 1]| is at most
// Succ^s-pd-ow(q). Proof: the two sides answer identically until the first
// query equal to sStar, so their distance is at most Pr[first hit]; that
// probability is determined by the view before the hit, which on either
// side is (pk, y) plus constant oracle answers; an adversary in that view
// that outputs its query set is a set PD-OW adversary with success
// Pr[first hit]. In the OAEP lemmas Solve is called on every HashH query,
// so q is the adversary's number of H-queries, as in FOPS.
//
// The reveal oracle exists because a ProofFrog reduction cannot halt when
// it has won: the Left side of a hop must remain the same program as its
// neighbour after the hit, including answering H(sStar) consistently with
// the challenge, i.e. with rStar + tStar, which the reduction can only
// compute if it is told tStar. By the lemma, what is revealed after the
// first hit does not affect the assumption. Two Bool/value oracles rather
// than one tuple-returning oracle keep the composed games in the
// `if (challenger.Solve(x))` shape the engine canonicalizes best.
//
// FOPS further show (Theorem 8) that for RSA with k > 2 k0, partial-domain
// one-wayness follows from plain one-wayness by a lattice argument; that
// number-theoretic step is out of scope here and PD-OW is assumed directly.

import '../../Primitives/TDP.primitive';

Game Left(TDP F, Int n1) {
    BitString<n1> sStar;
    BitString<F.k - n1> tStar;

    [F.PublicKey, BitString<F.k>] Initialize() {
        [F.PublicKey, F.SecretKey] kp = F.KeyGen();
        sStar <- BitString<n1>;
        tStar <- BitString<F.k - n1>;
        return [kp[0], F.evaluate(kp[0], sStar || tStar)];
    }

    Bool Solve(BitString<n1> s) {
        return s == sStar;
    }

    BitString<F.k - n1> RevealT(BitString<n1> s) {
        if (s == sStar) {
            return tStar;
        }
        return 0^(F.k - n1);
    }
}

Game Right(TDP F, Int n1) {
    BitString<n1> sStar;
    BitString<F.k - n1> tStar;

    [F.PublicKey, BitString<F.k>] Initialize() {
        [F.PublicKey, F.SecretKey] kp = F.KeyGen();
        sStar <- BitString<n1>;
        tStar <- BitString<F.k - n1>;
        return [kp[0], F.evaluate(kp[0], sStar || tStar)];
    }

    Bool Solve(BitString<n1> s) {
        return false;
    }

    BitString<F.k - n1> RevealT(BitString<n1> s) {
        return 0^(F.k - n1);
    }
}

export as PDOW;
